WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.530
Thanks for coming to my talk.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.110
Really excited to kind of peel
back the curtains a little bit

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.027
on Quarto and show it off
to the world for some

00:00:12.027 --> 00:00:13.200
of the first times.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.700
My name is Tom Mock,
and if you want

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.117
to follow along
with these slides,

00:00:16.117 --> 00:00:20.700
you can do so at
rstd.io/quarto-curious.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.352
So really for the
longest time and why

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:24.060
I'm so excited about
talking about it now

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.643
is that for the longest time we
weren't talking about Quarto.

00:00:26.643 --> 00:00:28.680
We were kind of working
on it behind the scenes,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.440
getting things ready for
that first stable release.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.830
In April of this year,

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.980
we actually did our first
kind of beta release

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.770
or some of the
initial soft release.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.560
Alison Hill wrote up this
great talk or great blog

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.800
post called We Don't Talk
About Quarto Until Now.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.390
Alison was one of
the folks who used

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.765
to work with the
development team

00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:48.430
before she moved
over to Voltron Data.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.030
And it just really kind of
opened the floodgates to people

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.640
hearing about Quarto, started
talking a little bit on Twitter

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.580
or generally just
generating some hubbub.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.620
For the most part, people
were really excited,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.370
you know, they're just like,
"Oh, I hear about Quarto.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.240
"Let me try it out.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.050
I really like it."

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.290
So Kelly said, you know,
she stayed up all night

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.450
switching over our marketing
materials over to Quarto.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.170
And her kind of takeaway was
"It's pretty rad, my dudes,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:10.900
I like it."

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.413
So she's enjoying it.

00:01:12.413 --> 00:01:13.830
And there was even
some excitement

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.040
in the Python community.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.150
So if you're familiar with
Jeremy Howard or the nbdev

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.310
team or Fast.ai,
they're actually

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.650
moving some of their
work over into Quarto

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.757
and using that along
with Jupyter Notebooks.

00:01:24.757 --> 00:01:26.340
In fact, Hamel is
going to be talking

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.140
about that tomorrow
in his talk, talking

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.357
about the next generation
version of nbdev.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.940
Now, while there's
a lot of excitement

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.070
in both the R and
the Python community,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.297
there was also people who
had a few more questions.

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:39.130
And I think that's where
a lot of y'all are.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.463
Y'all are the Quarto curious
and you've come to my talk

00:01:41.463 --> 00:01:44.710
to hear a little bit about, what
is Quarto? Why should I care?

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.170
What's different
about it? What's

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.390
going on with RMarkdown
and all of that?

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.285
First off, don't worry,
RMarkdown is not going away.

00:01:51.285 --> 00:01:52.660
We're still going
to maintain it.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.380
If bug fixes happen,
we'll definitely fix them.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.270
Ultimately, the question
that people have is

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.530
When can I switch to Quarto?

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:00.250
When is it ready?

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.410
Is it stable?

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.602
What's going on with it?

00:02:03.602 --> 00:02:05.310
So ultimately, we'll
answer that question

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.260
by saying, what is Quarto?

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.509
So Quarto is the next
generation version

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.960
of RMarkdown for everyone.
And that everyone kind of

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.533
has an asterisk to it in
terms of not 6 billion people

00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:16.950
or whatever's
around in the world,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.020
but for really any
data science language

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.530
or any future language.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.920
As JJ talked about in
his keynote this morning,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.910
we want to build a
100-year company.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.760
And while that today
might mean working

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.860
in R and Python and
Julia and JavaScript

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.810
and other languages,
in the future,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.970
we want kind of a
language-agnostic tool

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.240
that can work with anything,

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.392
anything that becomes
popular in the future.

00:02:37.392 --> 00:02:39.600
To make it a bit more clinical
instead of just saying

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.510
the next generation of R
Markdown, Quarto, if we define

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.750
it, is an open-source, scientific
and technical publishing system

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.100
built on Pandoc.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.110
So Pandoc is a
command line tool

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.507
that's also used by RMarkdown.

00:02:50.507 --> 00:02:52.590
So again, there's going
to be a lot of familiarity

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.330
as we go through these
slides, and we'll

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:55.967
build up a mental
model of how Quarto

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.050
is a little bit different
than RMarkdown and some

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.290
of the improvements.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.480
Quarto itself is a
command line interface,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.020
or what is called a CLI.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.640
So you can access Quarto via your
terminal, whether you are in RStudio

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.180
or any text editor or any
terminal on your computer.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.458
And this example, I'm just
calling quarto --help to see

00:03:13.458 --> 00:03:15.500
what can I do with Quarto
once I've installed it

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.595
on my machine. It'll print
out something like this

00:03:18.595 --> 00:03:19.970
and I've abbreviated
it so we can

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.448
see the slides nice and big.

00:03:21.448 --> 00:03:23.240
But the commands here
that we're looking at

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.180
are I can use quarto
render, to render documents

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.450
from the plaintext format
out to say HTML or PDF

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.070
or to a website.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.470
I can use quarto preview
what you'll actually

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.840
render the document and then
maintain a background web

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:36.360
server.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.020
So as I make changes, it
shows those in real time

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.150
on the actual rendered output.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.980
And because it's a
publishing system,

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.490
I cannot only render it locally,
but I can publish it to the web

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:49.460
places like GitHub pages or Netlify
or something else we'll talk

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.470
about in the future
called Quarto Pubs.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.720
To really kind of build up
our mental model, though,

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.970
and help differentiate a
little bit between RMarkdown

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:58.600
and Quarto,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.030
we need to kind of talk
about what RMarkdown is

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.160
and what is happening
behind the scenes.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.290
When you use RMarkdown
in your daily work,

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.060
you're probably just
thinking, Oh yeah,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.310
I open up an R
Markdown inside RStudio

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:11.920
type some code, type some
text, and then I render it

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.732
and I get something beautiful
out. Behind the scenes,

00:04:14.732 --> 00:04:16.690
or things that are
happening here in the middle,

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.709
is actually a
little bit of magic.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:20.769
It's not real magic,
it's code, but it's

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:23.500
things that are happening
as intermediary steps.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.330
So when your render
your R Markdown,

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.450
the R code is evaluated by
a package called knitr

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.060
that will evaluate R code,
generate graphics, generate

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.420
tables, embed HTML widgets
and all the other magic

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.620
that you're building.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.030
This generates an intermediary
Markdown file or a .md file

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.950
that is then passed
to pandoc, which

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:45.010
actually converts a
plaintext .md into something

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:45.890
like a report.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.750
So a word document, a
PDF report, or a HTML page

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.820
or a presentation just
like the presentation

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.800
I'm giving today,
something in HTML,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.210
or a more complex project
like a website or a book.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.800
So this process
is great and it's

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.140
been around for almost 10
years. You know, knitr is an old package.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:03.770
It's robust.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.500
We've been using it a long time.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.150
Something to note,
though, is that it

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:10.030
has this hard dependency on
R. So to use RMarkdown,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.640
whether you're using R
or other languages, you

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.820
have to have to have R,
you have to knitr, you have to have Markdown.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:18.700
And some of the formatting
or preprocessing that occurs

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.850
to the document actually occurs
with literal R code written

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.450
in the RMarkdown
package. For Quarto,

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:26.685
we have a similar diagram.

00:05:26.685 --> 00:05:28.810
And for some of you, if
you're not looking closely,

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:31.330
this might look like
the exact same workflow,

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.400
but there's a few
differences we'll note.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.890
Number one, rather
than using a .Rmd,

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:39.470
I'm now using a .qmd or a
Quarto Markdown document.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.980
It's going to feel almost
identical to R Markdown,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.930
which is great, and we're
still using knitr.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:48.070
So knitr will evaluate
our code from our .qmd,

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.570
generate an intermediary
Markdown file,

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.970
but now we have another
word added to pandoc.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.160
We have pandoc
with Lua filters.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:59.080
So we've moved any preprocessing
or post-processing into Quarto,

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:02.620
that command line interface,
or to affect them with pandoc

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:06.760
rather than having to call them
from R. What this does is still

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.130
allows us to create the
same reports, presentations

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.590
and projects that
are available, but we

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.570
don't have to have R in
the system to use this.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.720
You could use Quarto
on a fresh computer

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.580
and not have R
anywhere in it and just

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.610
render Markdown as opposed to
code. So people could use it

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:24.280
for scientific communication
without actually

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.020
having to execute code.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.110
But because, again, we're
trying to become this company

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.120
that is building for
all of data science

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:34.870
and building for scientific
communication in general,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:38.480
we want to do more than
just evaluate for R code.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.520
So Quarto also extends to
other languages.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.870
So Quarto can now use
Jupyter and Jupyter Kernels

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.480
as the engine rather
than just R And knitr.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.970
So you can imagine Jupyter
Kernels include things

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:51.690
like Julia or Python.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:52.980
We also have JavaScript

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.010
you can execute via Quarto.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:58.040
So you have this availability
of not just being limited to R

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.200
but collaborating with
other people in Python

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:03.230
or if you're a Python dev
yourself, also using Python

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.360
within your Quarto documents.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.280
And again, the output
that you're creating

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.330
is not any different.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:09.830
You're just using
a different engine

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.110
because you're using a
different programming language.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:13.730
And if I'm using
Python, all I have

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.330
to have is Python on my system.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.030
If I'm using Julia, all I have
to have is Julia on my system.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.970
And I can switch between
them if I wanted to

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:23.390
without having to change
the overall document.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.940
For some other people who
are working with, say, Julia

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.950
or Python or familiar
with Jupyter,

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:30.900
they don't want to use
a plaintext format.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.520
They might not even use RStudio.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.550
They want to use Jupyter
Notebooks or Jupyter

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.650
Lab as their environment,
and that's possible too.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.190
So you can use a
literal .ipython notebook,

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.720
or what's called
a Jupyter Notebook,

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.610
and whether you're
using Julia or Python,

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.630
you can execute
code inside of that

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.630
and with essentially the
same rendering process,

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.610
create the exact same output.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:53.570
So they're using this plaintext
.qmd or a more complex kind

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.910
of binary format like
Jupyter, all of those

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:57.620
can create the
same presentations,

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.300
the same reports,
and the same projects

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.460
that you want to create.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.940
If we think of what .qmd looks
like a little bit to what

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.930
your working mental model of,
say an R Markdown document,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.480
we have the YAML header,
or the metadata that

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.470
says do this with my document.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.550
So format: html, create
an HTML static page.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:20.940
Here we have side by
side choice of choosing

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.420
an engine for knitr in R or say
choosing an engine of Jupyter

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:28.540
or a Python 3 or Julia
to execute that code.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.430
And again, you're
executing it natively.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:33.539
You're not wrapping it in R
code or wrapping it in Python code

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:35.140
to execute the opposites.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.970
You're just evaluating it
in its normal engine.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.270
You can then write some code.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.980
So we have here a group_by()
summarize() with mtcars and dplyr

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.800
and then in a Python library
called siuba, both of which

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.560
look relatively similar,
but one's evaluating purely

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.320
in R and one purely in Python.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.420
Then we have a bunch
of text or Markdown

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.230
that we're using to, you know, create
the actual prose or the text

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.500
inside the document.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.290
And you can see that
this spans both columns.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.650
There's no difference in
the Markdown you're writing

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:06.330
or the way that you
use Quarto to kind

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:07.780
of typeset your document.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:11.040
That is the same between
any language you're using.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.070
So again, whether you're
using R or Python or Jupyter

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.720
or whatever else, you can
write these documents.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.450
And again get the
exact same output,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.550
whether you're changing
your engine or not.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:22.445
Cool! So we got a
plain text format.

00:09:22.445 --> 00:09:24.070
It looks kind of
similar to RMarkdown.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:24.983
I'm still not sold.

00:09:24.983 --> 00:09:25.900
What's the difference?

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.570
So let's talk about that.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.970
Number one, Quarto is
one install, what

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:31.890
we call "batteries included".

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.440
So when you actually install
the latest version of RStudio

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.090
that was released on Monday,
Quarto is just there.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:38.770
It's installed with RStudio.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.220
So as soon as you download
the newest version of RStudio

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.170
or upgrade it in the future,
you have access to Quarto.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.850
You don't have to
install anything else.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:47.100
You can just start using it.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.950
You would need an engine,
if you wanted to use R,

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.620
then you install R. If you wanted
to use Julia, you install Julia

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.450
or python, you install Python.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.150
But for at least RStudio users,
it's baked in to using it.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:09:58.650
So great!

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:01.233
It's always around and you never
really have to think about it

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:02.650
or have to update your packages.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.757
It's just there.

00:10:04.757 --> 00:10:06.840
The other half of that
"batteries included" though,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:08.610
is that there's a
whole bunch of formats

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.140
that have been
created over the past

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:12.450
5 or 10 years of RMarkdown.

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.510
So you have your basic formats
like HTML documents or Word

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.880
documents, PDF,
different presentations

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.000
like this that are made in
reveal.js or even an advanced layout

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.020
like distill.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.390
That's several different
R Markdown packages. In Quarto,

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:26.410
it's just all there.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:28.230
That one install of
Quarto brings along

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:30.930
all the different
formats and also includes

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.470
the even more complex formats.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.920
So things like websites
or blogs or books

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.990
or interactive
documents, all of that

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.428
can also be done just with
the one install of Quarto.

00:10:39.428 --> 00:10:41.970
So rather than having to manage
a bunch of different R Markdown

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.280
ecosystems that were
developed at different times,

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:45.970
it might have different syntax,

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:49.733
you have batteries included one
install with a shared syntax.

00:10:49.733 --> 00:10:51.150
There's still a
few things we want

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.190
to add into Quarto to match
full parity with R Markdown.

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.340
Specifically, flexdashboard is one
of the ones that we're

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.240
really passionate
about adding quickly.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.750
And that's a way of creating
dashboards with Quarto

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:04.740
as well as the older
version of flexdashboard in R Markdown.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:06.880
Great! So, it's a little
bit easier to install.

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:09.930
It might even be installed
automatically for you.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.580
The other part is
that you want to stay

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.480
in the comfort of
your own workspace.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.150
If I asked you to
bake a cake and I

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:16.920
told you to go do it in
the corporate kitchen here,

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.128
it might be really hard
versus going to your own home

00:11:19.128 --> 00:11:21.840
and baking it at your own
kitchen table, probably

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.580
a bit easier.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.310
Similarly with Quarto,
you get to choose and stay

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:28.350
within the comfort of
your own workspace.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.460
So you can obviously
use it inside RStudio.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:31.980
It's pre-installed there.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.020
It's going to again feel
almost identical to RMarkdown.

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.440
So all of that work you've
done to learn RMarkdown,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.990
or if you're learning RMarkdown or Quarto
for the first time,

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.620
learning Quarto can be applied
within the RMarkdown or sorry,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.510
within the RStudio ecosystem.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.200
You still have the button for render.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:48.993
You don't have to
go and use the CLI,

00:11:48.993 --> 00:11:50.910
but it's still there for
you, and it's actually

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:52.800
embedded into different
functions that

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.320
are available through RStudio.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:56.880
But again, when
you say, "Oh, I want

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.860
to collaborate with my
colleague or my friend,"

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.810
and they're saying,
"Oh, I work in Julia

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:03.010
"and I don't really
like using RStudio.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.520
"Like that's not the editor I
want to use," or "I'm a Python dev

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.460
and I really want to work
in a Jupyter Notebook."

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.920
Quarto works just as well
in a Jupyter notebook,

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:13.980
so you can use that as
your editor if you want to.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.680
Again, we've got kind of on
the left this idea of that's

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.490
the IPython notebook
you're working in.

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:21.320
You're writing pure, normal
Jupyter Notebook code.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:23.320
The only thing that's
different is that at the top

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.200
you now have a raw
chunk with the YAML

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:26.800
that's defining
what's the format?

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.130
What do you want to effect?

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.460
And then on the right you
have a Quarto preview.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:31.860
So when you're
typing and saving,

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.330
it'll actually
re-render and show you

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.600
the output instantly
as it builds it out.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.010
And again, maybe you
don't like using Jupyter.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:41.010
Maybe that's not what
you're wanting to look at.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.715
Maybe you use VS
Code as your editor

00:12:42.715 --> 00:12:44.340
and you're really
passionate about that

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:47.760
because you also work in, say,
JavaScript or other languages.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.760
We actually developed a
Quarto extension for VS Code.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.760
So you get this rich
interactive experience

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:55.510
both in a notebook format--

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:57.990
So, using again a plain .qmd
as opposed

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:01.590
to a Jupyter Notebook-- and
you can also do quarto preview

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:04.230
and get that real
time output as well.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.048
So whatever editor
you want or whatever

00:13:06.048 --> 00:13:08.340
editor you have to collaborate
with your colleagues on,

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:10.507
they can stay in the comfort
of their own workspace.

00:13:10.507 --> 00:13:12.465
You can stay in your own
comfortable workspace,

00:13:12.465 --> 00:13:15.030
and you can still collaborate
across a document with a shared

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.500
syntax.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:18.620
The other part of what

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.320
building for
RStudio and building

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.210
for other editors
like VS Code is

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:24.820
we have things like rich
autocompletion, which wasn't always

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:27.730
available and is something
that's added in Quarto.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:30.010
So, I'm editing a document
I might remember,

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:33.530
Oh yeah, I'm using format HTML,
but what else can I do here?

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.460
And if I click Control Space
inside RStudio or VS Code,

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.470
it'll actually pull up all
the options that are possible.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.823
Almost like with R
code inside the editor,

00:13:41.823 --> 00:13:43.240
you can hover over
it and see what

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:46.450
does this function actually
do. That works in YAML

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:48.320
and it also works
in chunk options.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.597
Again, there's dozens of
chunk options you can do,

00:13:50.597 --> 00:13:52.180
and while some of
them are very niche,

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:53.750
you're not going to
use them all the time.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:56.020
Those are very powerful for
knowing exactly what you

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:58.460
need to do and seeing like,
oh, I need to change figures.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:00.610
I just start typing figures
and it will autocomplete

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:01.845
to what's available.

00:14:01.845 --> 00:14:03.220
And again, whether
you're working

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:06.400
in Python or R or
RStudio or VS Code,

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.980
this is available for you.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:12.490
Great, so we've got some
autocompletion in Python

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:15.357
and R, maybe we want to
talk more about formats

00:14:15.357 --> 00:14:16.940
and we want to get
excited about that.

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:18.470
So this is actually
an HTML widget.

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:19.940
You'll notice that
I'm pulling it over.

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:21.440
This is actually
where we are today.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:22.970
So we're at the National Harbor.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:26.050
I can click on this and it
says "We are here, together!"

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:27.830
So I can drag that
over a little bit.

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:29.740
It'll load. The internet's
a bit bad in here,

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:31.250
so it's not loaded all the way.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:32.958
But importantly, you
can see I'm actually

00:14:32.958 --> 00:14:34.870
interacting with it with R code.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:37.210
So you can do Shiny,
you can do HTML widgets

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.850
or other things you've built
and put those into Quarto.

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:42.130
And in Python or your
Python colleagues,

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:44.600
they can also embed what
are called Jupyter Widgets.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:46.330
So similarly, you
have the ability

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:49.360
to create interactivity
on the client side,

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:52.750
both with Python and with R
within a single Quarto document

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.650
or in separate Quarto documents.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.350
Another part of
interactivity that Quarto adds,

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:00.860
though, is beyond just
using R and Python.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:02.730
It even embeds a
version of JavaScript

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.710
called Observable JavaScript.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.410
This is an extension
to vanilla JavaScript

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.998
created by Mike Bostock,
who's also the author of D3,

00:15:09.998 --> 00:15:12.540
that might be one you're more
familiar with in the JavaScript

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:13.500
land.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:16.650
And now we have a reactive
JavaScript library

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:19.290
that might look to you like
a little bit like Shiny.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:23.100
I have the ability to create
and interact with different tags

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:25.360
and it will actually
change the graphic.

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.390
Or I could do things
like filter the data

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:31.020
to have specific bill lengths
for this penguin's data set.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:32.760
And again, this is
baked into Quarto,

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:34.900
so you don't have to
install another thing.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:40.110
This interactivity is added
for the document itself.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.990
So we've got this kind of
idea of the interactivity,

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:43.960
different editors.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:47.190
We can also kind of unify a lot
of the syntax that was created

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.170
across years of RMarkdown.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.450
So again, this is
next-gen RMarkdown,

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:54.280
RMarkdown itself is not
being uninstalled from the web

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:55.530
and removed from the internet.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:56.740
We're still maintaining it.

00:15:56.740 --> 00:16:01.170
We'll fix any bugs, but our
new dev kind of effort

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.520
is going to go towards
quarto and to knitr.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:06.990
So knitr will still power
RMarkdown, still power Quarto,

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.810
but we're focusing on Quarto
as this next generation

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:14.937
of data science and technical
science communication.

00:16:14.937 --> 00:16:16.770
You might be asking
yourself, OK, well, I've

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:18.900
got a lot of old RMarkdowns
that I want to use.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:20.442
So for some of you,
keep using them.

00:16:20.442 --> 00:16:22.150
We're still going to
maintain RMarkdown.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:23.230
You don't have to switch

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:24.105
if you don't want to.

00:16:24.105 --> 00:16:25.380
You can keep using it.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.560
But Quarto can actually
render R Markdown documents

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.690
so you can take your existing
RMarkdown documents

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:34.350
and render them over to whatever
format you want via Quarto.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.210
So whether you're
staying in RMarkdown

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.890
or switching them over, you
can still render them out.

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:42.853
And in fact, this unification
of different formats,

00:16:42.853 --> 00:16:44.270
let's say we were
trying to render

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:47.630
to HTML and to PDF. For
HTML, we use things like CSS

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:49.410
and raw HTML. For PDF,

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:52.100
we're going to be using LaTeX
and other things like that.

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:54.770
Normally you'd have to write
a lot of manual code in there

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:56.060
to convert between them.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.460
But for this document, I'm not
writing any CSS or any LaTeX.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:02.540
I'm only using Quarto and I
can create a nicely typeset

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:03.733
document like this.

00:17:03.733 --> 00:17:05.150
It's got a picture
in the sidebar,

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:07.700
it's got a table of contents
and all sorts of other footnotes

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:08.839
and things.

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:11.569
That document in PDF
looks almost identical.

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:13.670
You're obviously constrained
to what PDF can do.

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:15.170
So you don't have
Hover Text and you

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:17.690
don't have the same
type of interactivity

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:20.630
as HTML, but without having
to write anything else

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:23.530
besides Quarto code
or Quarto Markdown,

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.060
I'm able to generate these two
very well typeset documents

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:27.150
from a single source.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:30.430
So single source
publishing for this.

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:32.930
And again, Quarto is extended
for other languages.

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.470
So this typesetting and all
the different things it brings

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:38.510
are made with Lua filters
rather than an R package.

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:41.680
So things that people
write in Python language

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:44.860
and in R or in Julia are
cross-language and cross-format

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.060
compatible.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:48.432
So we're meeting these users
in their native language.

00:17:48.432 --> 00:17:50.140
So when you are
collaborating, you're not

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:51.670
fighting with each
other, but you're

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:55.220
merging around a common syntax.

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.720
Those people might ask, well,
what to do about my existing

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:58.830
IPython notebooks?

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.420
I don't want to get rid of them.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:01.960
The answer is
essentially the same.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:05.880
I can quarto render any IPython notebook
and send it over to the format.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.580
The only difference is an
IPython notebook might actually

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:10.398
store computation inside of it.

00:18:10.398 --> 00:18:12.690
So you can either tell Quarto,
"Hey, take this document

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:17.460
and render it top to bottom," or
"Using the embedded execution,

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.070
"just format it out
to something else

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:23.180
or change the format
to the final output."

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.640
And in fact, Quarto
has a helper tool

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:29.720
to allow you to convert between
a plaintext .qmd and an

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:31.290
IPython notebook itself.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.160
So you can swap back and forth
whether you're using IPython notebooks

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:36.770
or Quarto without having
to change the source code,

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:40.530
you can actually convert
back and forth between them.

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:41.590
You can also extend

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:43.278
Quarto so we have
things like short codes.

00:18:43.278 --> 00:18:45.570
So if you wanted to add
something in to the open source

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:47.520
Quarto, you could
use a short code like this

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:50.190
that will insert things
like Font Awesome icons,

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.290
or you can add filters
that will actually

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.302
change the overall
appearance of a document.

00:18:54.302 --> 00:18:56.260
And again, this is not
limited to any language.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:58.740
So any extensions that you
write in a Python world

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.890
work in R and anything
in R works in Python,

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.940
you can also add
entirely new formats.

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:06.400
So maybe you want to create
a format for your company.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.070
You can create your own cool
company format for reveal.js.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:12.060
So these extensions
allow you to go further

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:16.270
with Quarto as we move
forward and add more things.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.490
And then lastly, I mentioned
this idea of Quarto Pubs,

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:21.250
so Quarto can actually
publish the thing you just

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.380
rendered to the
Internet, so you can

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:26.240
publish to GitHub or to
Netlify or RStudio Connect.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.940
But we're now announcing
that Quarto Pubs exist.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:30.140
It's a free resource.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:32.350
It allows you to publish
anything you create locally

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:34.248
and put that out on
the web at a URL

00:19:34.248 --> 00:19:36.040
you choose and manage
all of your documents

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:37.990
that you're publishing out.

00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:39.290
So this is very exciting.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:40.810
I would recommend
signing up quickly

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:44.950
to go grab your preferred
username and you can use that.

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:46.510
The last part I'll
say is, again, you

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:49.730
can use Quarto in RStudio Connect
and RStudio Workbench already.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:52.180
So if you do have those
products available for you,

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:53.710
we have some examples
of using them

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:55.690
and you're welcome
to go check them out.

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:58.460
And with that, we're getting
close to the end of the talk.

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:01.480
So I want to give a shout out
to the entire Quarto dev team.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:02.980
Quarto's crafted
with love and care

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:05.980
by the same team that works
on Quarto and RMarkdown,

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:08.483
and you can contribute
yourself if you

00:20:08.483 --> 00:20:10.150
want to contribute,
whether an extension

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:13.240
or to the Quarto CLI itself.

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.370
I've got a summary of the
talks that are available

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.590
and other things coming
up and some takeaways

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:20.470
with the batteries included,
the shared syntax, the ability

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:22.270
to choose your editor
and your language,

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:24.538
and the fact that RMarkdown
is not going anywhere.

00:20:24.538 --> 00:20:26.080
We're still maintaining
it, but we're

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.260
going to be building in a lot
of our new features into Quarto.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:30.992
With that, thank
you for your time.

00:20:30.992 --> 00:20:32.950
I'm happy to take questions
after the fact down

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:35.117
on the lounge or you can
reach out to me on Twitter.

00:20:35.117 --> 00:20:38.790
Thank you, and have a
great and safe conference.

